Note = {2nd International Conference on Heritage and Sustainable Development,
   Evora, PORTUGAL, JUN 22-26, 2010},
Organization = {Turismo Portugal; Fullservice Co; Viagens Abreu},
Abstract = {Australia has a well established heritage listing process however it is
   narrowly focused and remains based on the initial models adopted in the
   1960s. In both heritage and sustainability spheres there is currently
   little consideration for the potential value of heritage items and
   existing buildings as sustainable structures. This paper will explore
   the impact that sustailiability tools and legislation have on heritage
   items and 20(th) century buildings, with particular reference to Sydney.
   In a property environment driven by a free market economy will the
   sustainability imperative achieve what heritage legislation is so often
   unable to achieve conservation of high quality existing structures? The
   Green Building Council of Australia's (GBCA) ``Green Star Rating{''}
   tool has gained traction as a preferred measurement and management
   standard. This rating tool does not currently provide differentiation
   between heritage structures and other existing structures, however some
   adaptive reuse heritage projects have already been successfully rated
   under the Green Star system, and examples have been provided. I have
   also discussed the likely common aims and objectives for heritage and
   sustainability, and highlighted the current obstacles.},
